==Question #5==
{{Cquote2|What new ideas or pieces of content would you love to see in the game, or what changes would you love to see implemented for existing content?}}

[https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bHS1F5tx9xU&t=879s Youtube Time Stamp]

* '''Mod Bruno:''' I'll give my standard answer of the Completionist Cape. I'd love to have Completionist Cape added to the game at some point, where we can just give people a list of everything to complete and then people can go complete them and brag about them.
* '''Mod Ash:''' It may not count as being in the game, but I'd like a lobby! There's been some pushback for it before when it's been discussed, but you've already got the ''Message of the Week'' screen with info on it and links to useful stuff about your account, like how much credit you have left. The fact that you're in-game while reading that screen is not helpful for anybody. If anything, it might be a bit dangerous.
* '''Mod Ash:''' I'd prefer that you'd be in a lobby - we could add chat, so you can chat with other players in there - and you could interact with that screen a lot more safely without the game being hidden behind it with things potentially going on around you. We can disable the 'Message of the Week' screen in the most dangerous areas, but a lobby is still something I've always wanted.
* '''Mod Ash:''' You might find that we build you a better world-switching menu than the login screen currently has. If it were built into a lobby where we can actually script rather than being restricted to - respectfully - what the game engine had in 2007.
* '''Mod Ash:''' Apart from that, if we're looking at changes to existing content, I want a pop-up in the bank that would let you change the contents of your Rune Pouch flexibly while the bank is open to put things in and out of it.
* '''Mod Kirby:''' Quests! You know Slug Menace? I want a sequel to Slug Menace! Various other quests too, but honestly, I really liked the concept of Sea Slug and Slug Menace. I'd like a bit more there.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' Just not the same sequel where you use the pillar to kill the final exciting boss.
* '''Mod Ash:''' I think it would be appropriate if we have such a fight that there is a pillar with a push option. You push it, it falls, and it lands on her, and she's fine! She's actually really powerful, and she says, ''Well, what do you think that was gonna do?''. I'd like to see that.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' Something completely wild. I'm interested in this concept of being able to own buildings or plots in the real world.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' Now, this feature is sometimes present in other games, it wouldn't be unique to Old School. It would be completely different to anything we've done before in Old School. I imagine a plot of land somewhere in the world that goes to auction, you could bid with gold, and the highest bid wins.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' We sink that gold, so it's great for a gold sink. Then, you get this spot that you can customise and do various things with - imagine a version of the Achievement Gallery, where your achievements are on the actual maps and others can see them.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' I'm not sure how players would feel about that concept; it's not something I'm saying we're going to be doing. It's just a really interesting idea of how we could bring something more multiplayer-focused and world-changing based on the events of what the actual community does.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' It's slightly different to POH. In essence, it is a customisable house, so there is a common overlap, but being able to put it on the world map where somebody can see it when they're just wandering around the game world. Something that would be somewhat similar to Final Fantasy XIV, that kind of concept.
* '''Mod Light:''' This idea occurred to me when I was attending an event from a player who was organising a march. I struggled because I couldn't see the actual player who was organising the event amongst the masses of people within the game. So, what I thought would be really handy was if there was some way of making event organisers visually distinct - like a tag - just so that you can see them better. Alternatively, when they're chatting in the game, we could see what they're writing because, obviously, they're probably going to write something related to the event, and there are a lot of players talking a lot when you're attending an event. This could be very helpful when organising events!
* '''Mod Light:''' As for existing content, I'd love to see an incentive for people in the PvP Arena. There are a lot of complaints about the lack of rewards - I know that they failed the poll several times, which has affected player sentiment about it. I had a lot of fun when I play-tested the PvP Arena myself, even as someone who's super casual, so it's a shame that the PvP Arena doesn't have the rewards incentive as much to it. If we could ever agree on good rewards, that would be nice, and this is something that I'd love to change, but I don't know if it's possible.

==Question #6==
{{Cquote2|There has been a lot of concern from the PvP community about the way that PvP rewards get polled. Players are worried that perfectly legitimate items will fail the poll due to spite voting. What are your thoughts on this in light of the recent Wildy Boss Rewards? Can we restrict all PvP questions in the future, or even put them into the game without pulling them at all? You already have a PvP discord for legit feedback. }}

[https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bHS1F5tx9xU&t=1308s Youtube Time Stamp]

* '''Mod Kirby:''' Conveniently, I believe that Ayiza gave some prerequisites in the WBR blog. One: we can still balance the bosses themselves to have good GP per hour with or without the bonus rewards. Two: while the current rewards are good, if these fail, we can just rework them and come back to them at a later time, as we did with the current Revenant weapons as they exist in the game.
* '''Mod Kirby:''' Also, these Revenant weapons aren't just PvP weapons - we're putting a Tier 75 beatstick in there!  That will be good for all players, regardless of what you're actually doing in terms of actual activity in the game. Restricting it to a PvP poll isn't exactly appropriate, and as we've seen in the past, just restricting is no guarantee it's going to pass either. The PvP Arena rewards failed, despite being restricted and having a lot of feedback go through the PvP Discord as well.
* '''Mod Kirby:''' In this instance, it's nice that there's not too much negativity towards the rewards themselves. So, I'm confident that rewards are in a good space overall, even with the open polling, but I don't think restricting them or forcing them through unpolled is a healthy way to go about things.

==Question #7==
{{Cquote2|Are there any plans to address Phosani's Nightmare? It's one of the toughest bosses in the game, but GP/hr is now lower than far easier content. Why would mains kill this boss rather than the Giant Mole? }}

[https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bHS1F5tx9xU&t=1450s Youtube Time Stamp]

* '''Mod Light:''' We talked about this earlier in the week - we have a Weekly Suggestions Meeting where we discuss player suggestions. The consensus was that Nightmare should not be a cash cow piece of content. We need to strike a balance with this because of the number of uniques coming from Nightmare. 
* '''Mod Light:''' We do think that the price of uniques has been affected by ToA. There is an option to sink more through the GE tax, but uniques will lose their appeal unless we potentially buff them in a unique way. We could look at putting the Harmonised Orb in a better place, but in order to do this, we might look at changing the normal Spellbook. Standard spells currently don't scale with level, so if we rework that, that might help with the price of the Harmonised Orb. 
* '''Mod Light:''' We'll keep this in mind, but in terms of straight-up buffs, we won't be able to do anything at this stage. We'll probably look at it in terms of a Game Jam project.
* '''Mod Bruno:''' I think it's in an unfortunate position where a slight power creep has made the uniques from Nightmare less useful than they are worth because of how long it takes to get them. So, items like the new staff from the Tombs and Torva from Nex can make those unique items less good. They're still good but not as best-in-slot as they used to be. I'm not sure how we should fix or update that, but I think it should be improved.
* '''Mod Kirby:''' I think the staff's problem is that it's heavily tied to the standard Spellbook. You are required to pair it with a Tome of Fire, which restricts you from what you can equip it with. You can't take a Spectral or anything with you at the same time.
* '''Mod Kirby:''' Those two just get out-classed by, say, a new raid reward plus any given shield item; you can see where the loss of value comes from. Phosani's Nightmare, in my opinion, is still probably the best solo piece of content we have in terms of just an individual boss fight, but that might just be because I helped make it!
* '''Mod Kieren:''' At the end of the day, the item's price is a result of how rare it is - the supply and the demand in terms of how good it is. You can see the price of the Harmonised Orb right now has fallen quite a lot, and this is an item that is quite rare compared to other uniques from other bosses. We all know that Nightmares' drop rates are very low compared to Graardor, and you get something every 120-odd kills.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' That's where I feel the issue lies. It's just too niche, it's good at the specific thing it's good at, but it's just not wide enough that it can carry a better drop rate or a much better price.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' We don't know what the solution is overall, other than potentially sinking more for the G, but it's certainly a conversation in trying to work out: ''How can we make either the Harmonised Orb better or make for Phosani's Nightmare worth doing in a way that isn't just bulking out the regular drop table?''
* '''Mod Ash:''' In the meantime, though it is only a stopgap and not a great solution, we do intend to sink more via the GE tax in the short term while we look at possibilities for what would make it more desirable.

==Question #8==
{{Cquote2|Any chance the team would revisit the idea of changing Shopscape for irons? One of the most tedious and pointless things in the game is having to hop a 100 worlds so you can stock up on runes. }}

[https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bHS1F5tx9xU&t=1702s Youtube Time Stamp]

* '''Mod Kieren:''' I agree. It's tedious. It's awful. It's not fun. So, as designers, we don't want people doing that. It's not fun for any Ironman to sit there hopping worlds, competing with other Ironmen for the same stock when the shops restock slowly, and the costs skyrocket when ten or so are purchased.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' We did improve the shop of Lunar Isle recently so that it doesn't scale the price up so fast when they buy the stock, which essentially just increases the amount of buyable stock in the game.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' It takes hours and hours of messing around with shops, and I think Gold Ore is one of the worst culprits compared to runes. Ideally, it's more interesting if there's an interesting way to get these items. Think of the sand solution - we added the sand grinder, which made getting sand and even paying for it far more interesting than just saying, “here's a bunch of packs in the shop.”
* '''Mod Kieren:''' However, I think there's a balance to be struck. Until we add those better scaling systems and better solutions, we shouldn't be leaving it as painful as it is. This is my view, and I'd happily see more rune packs and stock increases. You're spending the gold anyway, so what's the point of making you spread it out over 4, 5, 6 hours of hopping worlds to buy them one at a time and getting frustrated when it's a busy time, and there are other people buying the stock up before you. It's just not fun, and we shouldn't leave our players to do something that tedious.
* '''Mod Light:''' I've seen a lot of comments about it myself, and I know that the dev team does want to encourage players to obtain runes in a more gameplay-oriented way. For Guardians of the Rifts, we wanted to encourage people to interact with the minigame in order to get the kind of runes that they wanted, and I think that was successful.
* '''Mod Light:''' It's clear that there's still an issue with shopscape for some Irons because they keep bringing it up to us. So, I'd be keen for any changes in that area. 
* '''Mod Ash:'''  It is hard for skilling activities to compete with the rates that the shop could pump them out. However, coin shops are not the only kind of shop available. One discussion that didn't get agreed on at the time but could have been revisited was whether the reward shop from Guardians of the Rift could sell packs of runes so that rather than going to the coin shop, you'd be playing a minigame and coming away with stacks of runes that way.

==Question #9==
{{Cquote2|Will the ToA design philosophy be used for most/all future content? That is, the concept of having a boss that is easy at its core, but which can be made more difficult by turning on optional mechanics? Will we ever see something like the inferno or ToB again?}}

[https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bHS1F5tx9xU&t=1923s Youtube Time Stamp]

* '''Mod Ash:''' That can certainly work with replayable content, and I do expect there'll be more use for it. We're very much aware of the interest in seeing Chambers of Xeric and ToB get some kind of scaling feature. You know how painful it is to customise your CoX grades with alts and scouts, etc. So a feature for customising it via a coin sink is well worth offering. 
* '''Mod Ash:''' In the few discussions I've had with players, it seems like something people would be willing to get behind even for quite a lot of coins, so that could be worth pursuing. Now that said, I also think there'll be a need for truly elite content in the game, and there's something about an Infernal Cape rather than ToA on a particular setting. While it may be as difficult and as great an achievement, it doesn't have the same ring to it because there are so many other versions available. So, I think there will be a need for truly elite content, so the best of the best can get their flex off. 
* '''Mod Ash:''' On the other hand, a project like ToA took nearly a year from quite a large chunk of the team. If we're going to put in that much time on something, and therefore they're not available to make other things for other players, we want the thing they're producing at the end of all that time to be something a lot of you can engage in. That means making it in a way that mid-level and above can plausibly have some fun with, and invocations and scaling options seem like a good way of offering that. This doesn't mean there can't be really elite stuff, but that might be more the size of the Inferno than a raiding dungeon. I doubt we do another thing on the scale of ToB that was so inaccessible to the casual player.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' The only thing I wanted to expand on a bit other than scaling was that ToB's death system was so harsh. It made it hard to get into the content and learn it because you think that you have to fight so many bosses, and then finally do Verzik. To practice Verzick was actually quite hard. So, you can do the first four in any order in the new raid, but you also have better ways to get to grips with it. 
* '''Mod Kieren:''' Anybody who is familiar with high-end raids, and not just Old School, and not just Runescape, but other titles as well know that finding teams is hard. You have this vicious circle of teams requesting only people who've done 50 kill counts, etc. How do you get your first kill count if you can't get a team because all the teams require you to already know it? That is a big problem games face. We want to make that a better journey and provide a journey that you can actually learn the content by and get into it and get to a higher level. 
* '''Mod Kieren:''' Ash covered it really well - raids are expensive to make. It's not only invocations - they aren't the only way to scale up content, and that might well be something we experiment more with in the future and offer it in different systems. Some content that Runescape has done boss-wise; there are enrage systems that scale up the more you defeat it, and you unlock higher enrage options. That essentially ends up being sort of a difficulty slider rather than a completely customizable ''choose your invocation'' feature.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' We could experiment with a lot, and we've learned already from doing invocations. Balancing them is really quite hard and takes a lot of effort, and I wouldn't say it's perfect by any means. So, we'll see what happens in future content.
* '''Mod Bruno:''' I think it's been very well made for the Tombs of Amascut. I definitely don't think we're gonna be doing it for everything, but we'll do it when it makes sense, like with a bigger raid or a bigger piece of content. Say we add a new boss, I wouldn't expect it to come out with one of those, though you might get one of the twists. I'm not going to say definitely because that's a strong word, but I think we'll most likely get something big like Theatre of Blood or the Inferno at some point in the future.
* '''Mod Kirby:''' I feel like having the scaling stuff is slowly going to become a requirement for group content, because we're going to get players with different proficiencies and experience levels with the game. For them to play together, you need to reliably have it at the lowest sort of denominator of who can actually access it. Obviously, that's gonna be different for different groups of people.
* '''Mod Kirby:''' You want your sweaty group to be able to go, "okay, we'd like to have all the invocations and die in two seconds to everything", but for getting Mod Light into a party, I think I'd like to turn the difficulty down a little bit - no shade.
* '''Mod Light:''' I love accessibility and for what it's worth, I think games thrive with both accessible content, but also flashy content that your top-level players can flip. I'm just glad that players received it so well. There was a bit of concern that our high-level players wouldn't accept it as much because it's something new, but actually, high-level players are seemingly really enjoying the new invocation system and having a lot of fun with it.

